docs/implementation-plan.md-1-# Curator implementation plan
docs/implementation-plan.md-2-
docs/implementation-plan.md:3:This is the working plan for building Curator, an agent environment manager (AEM) in Go. Iterations consult this document and the task board; the plan is updated as decisions land. Protocol sections are cited as `Spec §N.M` against the 1.0.0-rc.2 [Curator Specification](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec).
docs/implementation-plan.md-4-
docs/implementation-plan.md-5-## 1. Goal and definition of done
docs/implementation-plan.md-6-
docs/implementation-plan.md:7:Curator v0.1 is a conforming independent implementation of the protocol: it works with the same skill packages, the same project manifests, the same installed layouts, and the same audit registries as the reference implementation, per the eight conformance requirements of Spec §17.1. Done means:
docs/implementation-plan.md-8-
docs/implementation-plan.md-9-1. All conformance-relevant behavior is implemented and covered by tests, including the golden interoperability suite (Section 6 below).
docs/implementation-plan.md-10-2. `curator` installs a real project from `Skillfile.json` end to end on macOS, Linux, and Windows.
--
docs/implementation-plan.md-20-| Language | Go 1.23+ | Static binaries, first-class Windows, stdlib `crypto/ed25519` |
docs/implementation-plan.md-21-| Module | `github.com/relux-works/curator` | Public repository |
docs/implementation-plan.md-22-| CLI framework | stdlib `flag` with a small subcommand dispatcher | The CLI surface is informative in the spec; avoid framework lock-in. Revisit only if UX demands it |
docs/implementation-plan.md:23:| Git operations | shell out to system `git` with `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file:git:http:https:ssh`, URLs passed after `--` | Matches Spec §8.2 transport restrictions exactly; no libgit2/go-git divergence risk |
docs/implementation-plan.md-24-| JSON | stdlib `encoding/json` with strict unknown-field rejection where the spec requires it | Spec §5.1, §6.1 |
docs/implementation-plan.md-25-| TOML (codex configs) | `github.com/BurntSushi/toml` | Read-only MCP surface parsing (Spec §11.1) |
docs/implementation-plan.md-26-| JSONC (opencode) | minimal in-repo comment stripper, then stdlib JSON | One surface, trivial grammar; no dependency |
--
docs/implementation-plan.md-29-| TUI (later phase) | `bubbletea` + `lipgloss`; tests with `tuitestkit` from skill-go-testing-tools | Closed-loop TUI testing |
docs/implementation-plan.md-30-| Testing | stdlib `testing`, table-driven; authoritative vectors from `curator-spec/conformance/v1` | Deterministic and shared by independent implementations |
docs/implementation-plan.md-31-| Lint/CI | `gofmt`, `go vet`, `golangci-lint`, GitHub Actions matrix (ubuntu, macos, windows) | Windows is first class |
docs/implementation-plan.md:32:| Layout | `cmd/curator/` (main), `internal/<domain>/` packages mirroring protocol domains | Spec-traceable package boundaries |
docs/implementation-plan.md-33-| Machine home | `~/.curator/` (env `CURATOR_CONFIG`, `CURATOR_SYSTEM_CONFIG`; system config `/etc/curator/config.json`, `%ProgramData%\curator`) | The machine home is tool-specific state (config, caches, runtime store), not a wire format; two managers on one machine must not share it. Shared wire formats stay exactly as specified: `Skillfile.json`, canonical `agent-skill.json`, legacy `csk-skill.json`, markers, adapter ledgers |
docs/implementation-plan.md-34-
docs/implementation-plan.md-35-Package sketch: `internal/skillspec`, `internal/manifest`, `internal/devsub`, `internal/config`, `internal/identity`, `internal/gitops`, `internal/snapshot`, `internal/hashing`, `internal/closure`, `internal/whitelist`, `internal/locale`, `internal/marker`, `internal/runtimestore`, `internal/shims`, `internal/adapters`, `internal/mcp`, `internal/scopes` (global, hybrid, consumers, gc), `internal/registry`, `internal/audit`, `internal/shell`, `internal/cli`.
--
docs/implementation-plan.md-38-
docs/implementation-plan.md-39-- Tasks live on the in-repo board `.task-board/` (epics, stories, tasks as directories with `README.md` and `progress.md`; artifacts in `.task-board/.resources/<ID>/`). Every change starts from a task; progress and status are updated as work proceeds.
docs/implementation-plan.md-40-- Commits are discrete and meaningful: one logical step per commit, imperative subject, body explaining what and why. Signed (SSH key, verified on GitHub) as Ivan Oparin <oparin@me.com>.
docs/implementation-plan.md:41:- The name of the alternative protocol implementation appears exactly once: the README open-protocol section. A CI check greps case-insensitively (zero matches outside README.md, exactly one inside). The protocol is cited as `Spec §N.M` against [curator-spec](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec); protocol file names (`Skillfile.json`, `agent-skill.json`, legacy `csk-skill.json`, `.csk-install.json`, `.csk-managed.json`) are part of the wire format and are used as-is.
docs/implementation-plan.md-42-- Spec first: when implementation and spec disagree, stop and resolve the spec question before coding around it.
docs/implementation-plan.md-43-- Every error message that the spec words normatively (allowlist refusal, MCP hint, conflicts with chains) keeps the same information content, not necessarily the same string.
docs/implementation-plan.md-44-- Style for prose (docs, board cards): plain technical English, no em dashes, no guillemets.
--
docs/implementation-plan.md-51-
docs/implementation-plan.md-52-`go.mod`, `cmd/curator` hello skeleton with `--version`, Makefile (`build`, `test`, `lint`), GitHub Actions (test matrix + lint), golangci-lint config, `CONTRIBUTING.md` (working agreements from Section 3). Acceptance: green CI on all OS, `curator --version` prints a version.
docs/implementation-plan.md-53-
docs/implementation-plan.md:54:### Phase 1: protocol formats (EPIC protocol-formats)
docs/implementation-plan.md-55-
docs/implementation-plan.md-56-Parsing and validation, pure functions over bytes, no filesystem side effects beyond reads:
docs/implementation-plan.md-57-
--
docs/implementation-plan.md-106-
docs/implementation-plan.md-107-### Phase 8: audit gate (EPIC audit-gate)
docs/implementation-plan.md-108-
docs/implementation-plan.md:109:Minimal conforming audit layer (§12): enabled/mode/fail_on decision semantics (§12.2), local revocations by hash and source glob, operator pins, canary-as-blocking, a small deterministic detector set (capability-vs-observed for network hosts and exec names in scripts), verdict cache. Backends beyond a null backend are deferred; the decision semantics MUST hold.
docs/implementation-plan.md-110-
docs/implementation-plan.md-111-### Phase 9: shell and CLI polish (EPIC shell-and-cli)
docs/implementation-plan.md-112-
--
docs/implementation-plan.md-128-
docs/implementation-plan.md-129-- Unit: table-driven per MUST clause, typed error assertions.
docs/implementation-plan.md-130-- Integration: temp-dir projects with real git repositories built in test setup; full install cycles; idempotence (second install is `up-to-date`); tamper detection.
docs/implementation-plan.md:131:- Interop: the external versioned protocol suite (Phase 10) as the conformance gate.
docs/implementation-plan.md-132-- TUI: tuitestkit closed-loop (Phase 11).
docs/implementation-plan.md-133-- Windows: everything runs in the CI matrix; path handling tested with `win_path`, `.cmd` shims, `%ProgramData%` system config.
docs/implementation-plan.md-134-
docs/implementation-plan.md-135-## 7. Risks and watchpoints
docs/implementation-plan.md-136-
docs/implementation-plan.md:137:- Byte-compatibility of the content hash and marker JSON is the highest-risk surface; build Phase 2 hashing against the shared protocol vectors first.
docs/implementation-plan.md-138-- JSON field ordering in markers: the spec fixes content, not key order; markers are compared as parsed objects, hashes as bytes.
docs/implementation-plan.md-139-- Windows symlink permissions: adapter `auto` mode must degrade to copy without failing (Spec §10.1).
docs/implementation-plan.md-140-- Git version drift across CI runners: pin behaviors by testing outcomes, not stdout strings.
--
